{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Lemech took for himself two wives, etc.</b> This entire account of his two wives, Adah and Tzillah, carries a great necessity in the Torah — to know and to make known that when the union is proper, for the sake of perpetuating the species alone, then fitting children are born to them, keepers of the charge of HaShem; but when it is not for the perpetuation of the species but rather for the sake of beauty, to fill the bit of his desire, then alien children are born to them — seed of evildoers, children of destruction. As we find in these two wives, Adah and Tzillah: the one taken for procreation is called עָדָה, as Rashi explains; and it is also possible to explain it as the language of pregnancy, whose Aramaic translation is ״וְעַדִּיאַת״ (Genesis 4:1). And the one taken for intercourse alone is called צִלָּה, as Rashi explains; and perhaps they were at least modest in intercourse, always making a shade. If so, justice demands that from Adah fitting children would be born. This is what it says: ״וַתֵּלֶד עָדָה אֶת יָבָל הוּא הָיָה אֲבִי כָּל יֹשֵׁב אֹהֶל וּמִקְנֶה״ — for this was the craft of Abel the righteous, and after him all the upright of heart, such as Moses and David and many prophets and righteous ones, until through this craft they merited attachment to the Shechinah. And the name of his brother was Yuval; he was the father of all who play the lyre and the pipe, which were created to praise with them the Creator of the world, blessed be He, as it is written (Psalms 150:3): ״הַלְלוּהוּ בְּנֵבֶל וְכִנּוֹר״, etc. And through them too the spirit of prophecy rests, as it is written (II Kings 3:15): ״וְהָיָה כְּנַגֵּן הַמְנַגֵּן וַתְּהִי עָלָיו יַד ה׳״. But Tzillah, whom he took for intercourse — he gave her a cup of sterility and it availed her nothing; in any event, in the end alien children were born to him, as it says: ״וְצִלָּה יָלְדָה גַם הִיא אֶת תּוּבַל קַיִן״ — who restored the deed of Cain to its place and fashioned instruments of destruction to maim and to kill, as it says: ״לֹטֵשׁ כָּל חֹרֵשׁ נְחֹשֶׁת וּבַרְזֶל״ — that shortens a person's days. And perhaps this is measure for measure, for frequent intercourse shortens a person's days; therefore their offspring turned out like them, to engage in things that shorten the days, and his craft went forth into actual practice, as it says: ״כִּי אִישׁ הָרַגְתִּי לְפִצְעִי״, etc. And this is a correct allusion in the Torah, to learn from it the matter of union according to the Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:12.244160844Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}